# Tests

By now, you should be familiar enough with [unit testing](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unit_testing). Like anything, the more you do it, the better you will be at it. More importantly, you will begin to understand how to write code that is easier to test.

We'll use [TDD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Test-driven_development) for this part.

## Assignment

In this course we're going to use the [vitest](https://vitest.dev/guide/) testing framework, it's very popular and easy to setup.

1. [ ] Install Vitest:

```bash
npm install -D vitest
```

2. [ ] Add the `"test": "vitest --run"` to our `package.json` in the `"scripts"` object, it should look something like this:

```json
  "scripts": {
    "test": "vitest --run"
  },
```

3. [ ] Create a new file in `src`, I called mine `repl.ts`. Create a shell of a function called `cleanInput` that takes a string and returns an array of strings:

The purpose of this function will be to split the user's input into "words" based on whitespace. It should also lowercase the input and trim any leading or trailing whitespace. For example:

- `hello world` -> `["hello", "world"]`
- `Charmander Bulbasaur PIKACHU` -> `["charmander", "bulbasaur", "pikachu"]`

```ts
export function cleanInput(input: string): string[] {
  // logic goes here
}
```

4. [ ] Create a new file in `src` for some unit tests. I called mine `repl.test.ts`. (The only requirement is that it ends in `.test.ts`.) Create a test suite for the `cleanInput` function. Here is the basic structure of the test file:

**Import the `cleanInput` function:**

```ts
import { cleanInput } from "./repl.js";
```

**Import `describe`, `expect` and `test` from vitest:**

```ts
import { describe, expect, test } from "vitest";
```

**Use [`describe.each`](https://vitest.dev/api/describe#describe-each) to create a test suite:**

```ts
describe.each([
  {
    input: "  hello  world  ",
    expected: ["hello", "world"],
  },
  // TODO: more test cases here
])( // we'll do stuff here in a bit
```

**Write the logic for the tests by looping over each case:**

```ts
describe.each([
  {
    input: "  hello  world  ",
    expected: ["hello", "world"],
  },
  // TODO: more test cases here
])("cleanInput($input)", ({ input, expected }) => {
  test(`Expected: ${expected}`, () => {
    // TODO: call cleanInput with the input here

    // The `expect` and `toHaveLength` functions are from vitest
    // they will fail the test if the condition is not met
    expect(actual).toHaveLength(expected.length);
    for (const i in expected) {
      // likewise, the `toBe` function will fail the test if the values are not equal
      expect(actual[i]).toBe(expected[i]);
    }
  });
});
```

5. [ ] Once you have at least a few tests in your suite, run the tests using `npm run test` from the root of the repo. _We expect them to fail_.
6. [ ] Implement the `cleanInput` function to make the tests pass.

**Run and submit** the CLI tests.

# Help and Exit

A REPL is only useful if it does something! Our REPL will work using the concept of "commands". A command is a single word that maps to an action.

We're going to support two commands in this step:

- `help`: prints a help message describing how to use the REPL
- `exit`: exits the program

## Assignment

1. [ ] Remove your logic that prints the first word (the command) back to the user
2. [ ] Add a callback for the `exit` command. Commands in our REPL are just callback functions. I created a `command_exit.ts` file and added a function:

```ts
export function commandExit();
```

This function should print `Closing the Pokedex... Goodbye!` then immediately exit the program. I used [`process.exit(0)`](https://nodejs.org/api/process.html#process_process_exit_code) to exit the program.

3. [ ] Create a "registry" of commands. This will give us a nice abstraction for managing the many commands we'll be adding. I created a type that describes a command:

```ts
export type CLICommand = {
  name: string;
  description: string;
  callback: (commands: Record<string, CLICommand>) => void;
};
```

Then I created an object literal of supported commands:

```ts
export function getCommands(): Record<string, CLICommand> {
  return {
    exit: {
      name: "exit",
      description: "Exit the Pokedex",
      callback: commandExit,
    },
    // can add more commands here
  };
}
```

4. [ ] Register the `exit` command. Update your REPL loop to use the "command" the user typed in to look up the callback function in the registry. If the command is found, call the callback (and log any errors that are thrown). If there isn't a handler, just print `Unknown command`.
5. [ ] Test your program (obviously).
6. [ ] Add a `help` command, its callback, and register it. It should print:

```
Welcome to the Pokedex!
Usage:

help: Displays a help message
exit: Exit the Pokedex
```

Generate the "usage" section by iterating over my registry of commands. That way the help command will always be up-to-date with the available commands. I recommend:

- Put your handler callback functions in their own files (e.g. `command_exit.ts`, `command_help.ts`)
- Pass the command registry to the callback functions (although `exit` will ignore it) like this `export function commandHelp(commands: Record<string, CLICommand>)`
- Define the `CLICommand` type in its own file (e.g. `command.ts`)
- You can import types explicitly in TypeScript like this: `import type { CLICommand } from "./command.js";`

7. Test your code again manually.

**Run and submit** the CLI tests from the **root of the repo**.
